today i'm pleased to announce  nominations and staff for  critical foreign policy national  security positions key nominations  and appointments for the critical  economic positions i'm really pleased to  add members  to my team that'll get the job done it's  a rule of thumb in washington that  personnel is policy  that is to say a new president's choices  for top jobs in his administration  tell you an awful lot about where that  administration will be headed  we've now seen president-elect joe biden  make more than two dozen choices for top  jobs in the administration that will  take office in january  and that's enough to draw some  conclusions about the kind of team he's  put together  the first thing to say perhaps is that  mr biden clearly chose not to let the  dispute about the outcome of the 2020  election those lawsuits president trump  and his campaign  have filed to try to overturn the  results from getting in the way of  moving forward  on the personnel front you know under  similar circumstances in 2000 when the  outcome of the election again was under  dispute  the new president then george w bush  chose not to make choices for top jobs  until december  and some people think that put his new  administration behind the curve a little  bit at the outset  that's clearly a risk mr biden chose not  to take beyond that there are several  notable characteristics that mark the  emerging biden team  first it's center left not far left joe  biden campaigned and won  as a centrist democrat and against the  more progressive wing of his own party  and his team reflects that there's no  senator bernie sanders  there's no senator elizabeth warren  they're the favorites of democratic  progressives  instead mr biden has tended more toward  fellow veterans of the obama  administration  people with establishment credentials  and people who have worked with mr biden  for years  including his chief of staff ron clain  his national security adviser jake  sullivan  his white house counselor steve roshetti  his secretary of state tony blinken  and the latest addition to that list and  the surprising one susan rice who's  moved from being a national security  expert  to head of the domestic policy council  second this is not exactly a team of  rivals you know when barack obama won  the presidency in 2008  he famously picked his top democratic  primary opponent that year hillary  clinton  to be his secretary of state arguably  the most important person in his cabinet  i have known hillary clinton as a friend  a colleague  a source of counsel and a tough  campaign opponent she possesses an  extraordinary intelligence  and a remarkable work ethic i am proud  that she will be our next secretary of  state  well that hasn't been so much the  pattern here joe biden obviously picked  kamala harris a primary season opponent  to be his running mate  and he's just chosen pete buda judge the  former mayor of south bend indiana  and another opponent in the primaries to  be secretary of transportation  but most of the top jobs have instead  gone to people who joe biden has known  for years in other capacities such as  general lloyd austin  who was the leader of troops in iraq  when joe biden was vice president they  got to know each other then  he's picked to be now secretary of  defense third this team is diverse  mr biden said during the campaign and  repeatedly sensed the election that he  wants to pick a team that looks like  america  and his choices clearly reflect a desire  to do that  i said at the outset i wanted to  represent this campaign to represent and  look  like america we've done that now that's  what the administration will look like  and act like for all those of you  some firsts have been set here the white  house communications team will be made  up of all women  the treasury secretary will be a woman  her deputy will be a black man  the former head of the congressional  black caucus will be a top white house  advisor  the director of national intelligence  will be a woman for the first time  some on the left will say there should  have been more diversity but some  critics will say there  has been some diversity for diversity's  sake and they'll probably point to  general austin at the pentagon  or california attorney general javier  basara at the department of health and  human services despite the fact  he doesn't have a health or a medical  background fourth most of these picks  will not be controversial but a few may  be  it's rare for a new president not to get  the senate to go along with his top  choices to fill out his new  administration but it's not unheard of  the most notable exception in recent  times probably came in 1988  when the new president george h.w bush  picked a former senator john tower to be  a secretary of defense  and the senate rejected that pick i will  be recorded as the first cabinet nominee  in the history of the republic to be  rejected in the first 90 days of a  presidency  and perhaps be harshly judged if  republicans control the senate after  a couple of runoff races in georgia in  january  they'll be in a position to block  president-elect biden's picks if they  choose to do so and they may make  one or two choices to make that point  clear  one of the people who's a perhaps  vulnerable in that regard is near a  tandem  pick to head the office of management  and budget and who's riled up republican  senators with some of her partisan  comments over the years  as he finishes out his team it'll be  interesting to see whether mr biden goes  for a couple of republicans for offices  just to make real his pledge that he  wants to work across the aisle with the  other party  bottom line is that this team as is  often the case reflects the president  that has chosen it that is to say  it's long on washington experience and  center left
 

